All,

Status of CC ratings of different PE brands:

These can be expected to change as the market requirements change, as fitti=
ngs makers manage their suppliers of resin, and as the resin makers respond=
.  The coming update to AWWA C906 requiring CC3 to be published early 2022 =
will accelerate efforts by fittings makers to upgrade to CC3.


1.      Agru/CC0=1B$B"*"*=1B(BCC3

a.      Has changed to an unnamed compound whose CC testing has not been co=
mpleted.  See Borealis update below for related developments.

b.      They say their products are (or will be) CC3.  But the compound the=
y are using is still undergoing the testing they hope will establish a CC3 =
rating.  This testing will not finish until the end of the year.

c.      In the meantime their butt fusion and electro fusion fittings are s=
trictly speaking CC0 (meaning unrated).   I have asked them if the testing =
is far enough along to qualify for CC2 and/or can they write a certificatio=
n to a CC rating.

d.      It appears they changed from the Ineos material or this is a differ=
ent Ineos material.

e.      This applies to both butt fusion and electrofusion fittings.

2.      GF-Central Plastics/CC3

a.      Uses Ineos CC3 for molded products.

b.      Declares a blanket CC3 rating for all =1B$B!H=1B(Bblack=1B$B!I=1B(B=
 butt/socket fusion molded and electrofusion products but not for their fab=
ricated products at this time.  They have an intention for fabricated fitti=
ngs to be CC3 in time and can supply CC3 fabricated when needed.

3.      Imported brands/CC0

a.      Including Strongbridge, Nupi, Integrity, Plasson (EF) all currently=
 use PE compounds that are unrated thus CC0.  This can be expected to chang=
e in time.

b.      Integrity has recently started molding some flange adapters in Geor=
gia.  If they are using a proper domestic source for compound it would be e=
ither Ineos or Dow material, which are CC3 and CC2 respectively.  Their oth=
er butt fusion products are made in China and would be CC0.  Their electrof=
usion products are made in Italy and would be CC0.

c.      Strongbridge (Tega) uses Borealis material.   It has no rating so w=
ould be CC0.  See Borealis update below.

d.      This is on Nupi=1B$B!G=1B(Bs radar.  See Borealis update below.

4.      Rahn Plastics/CC2

a.      Uses Dow CC2.

5.      Performance Pipe/CC2

a.      Uses Dow CC2.

6.      JM Eagle/CC3

a.      Uses Ineos CC3.

7.      IPP/CC3 but can be CC2

a.      Use Ineos (CC3) as primary but also use Dow (CC2) as back up or for=
 competing procurement.  We will have to consider how to treat our procurem=
ent.

8.      Swan Plastics/CC3

a.      Bootlegs the Ineos by blending their own =1B$B!H=1B(Bsalt & pepper=
=1B$B!I=1B(B.

Update from Borealis:
Borealis is a European resin maker that is entering the US market whom we h=
ave been in touch with.  It will be =1B$B!H=1B(BFall=1B$B!I=1B(B before the=
 CC testing is completed for their new offering.  They expect a CC3 materia=
l.  They also will complete their PPI TR4 testing in the same time frame.  =
They will let us know when we can sample.  They will probably serve this ma=
rket from a European plant in the beginning.  This may be the new Agru mate=
rial.  It would also be a candidate for Nupi, Strongbridge, Integrity (EF m=
ade in Italy), Plasson (EF).

Update from Dow

Dow has a CC3 material but they are not making it available in pre-compound=
ed form yet.  They have not seen a demand for this material.  This should b=
e rectified by the end of the year.

We do not have a complete set of viable sources with CC3 ratings.  Recall G=
F is at extremely unfavorable pricing.

In the golf market CMF isn=1B$B!G=1B(Bt in a position to press CC3 because =
their PE ball valve uses Asian sourcing for PE compound and would not be CC=
 rated.

j

